  "Libraries of things have been spreading quietly for more than a decade. Most are run by volunteers or by public libraries, and they work in much the same way as a book library: members sign up, borrow an item for a week or two, and bring it back. Some charge a small yearly fee; others are completely free. Merrow’s library began with about eighty items donated by local residents. Five years later, it lends more than six hundred.",
  "The idea is simple, but its supporters believe it addresses several problems at once. The first is waste. Many household objects are used only a few times before being stored in a cupboard and eventually thrown away. A drill, according to one often-repeated estimate, is used for a total of only about thirteen minutes in its entire life. If ten households share one drill, nine fewer drills need to be made, shipped and finally sent to a landfill.",
  "The second problem is cost. For families with limited budgets, buying equipment for a single project or a single holiday can be out of the question. A borrowed tent can make a camping trip possible for a family that could never afford to buy one. “We see students borrowing projectors for presentations and parents borrowing baby equipment their children will outgrow in months,” says Ravi Menon, one of the volunteers in Merrow. “For them, it is not about saving the planet. It is about {uA|getting by}.”",
  "Supporters also point to a less obvious benefit: the library brings people together. Members often stay to chat when they return an item, and the library holds regular repair evenings where volunteers fix broken items and teach visitors how to do it themselves. Several members say they met neighbors there whom they had passed on the street for years without speaking.",
